  g.MODELS = (g.MODELS || []).concat([
    { id: 't2-p31', task: 2, ref: 'p31', band: '8.0', words: 285,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Since generative AI became [[freely available|được cung cấp miễn phí]], teachers have been [[struggling to decide|loay hoay quyết định]] whether tools such as chatbots belong in students' homework. In my view, they should be permitted, but only [[under clear conditions|với những điều kiện rõ ràng]] that keep the real thinking in the student's hands.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The strongest argument for allowing these tools is that they are already [[an integral part of|một phần không thể thiếu của]] modern working life. <<Giải thích>> Banning them in school would leave young people [[ill-prepared for|thiếu sự chuẩn bị cho]] workplaces where drafting, summarising and checking are routinely assisted by software. {{Moreover}}, a ban is almost impossible to enforce, so it mainly [[penalises honest students|gây thiệt thòi cho học sinh trung thực]] while others use the tools secretly. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, a pupil who asks an AI to explain a difficult reading passage in simpler language is [[making legitimate use of|sử dụng một cách chính đáng]] the technology, much as earlier generations used a dictionary or a tutor. Used in this way, AI can act as [[a patient personal assistant|một trợ lý cá nhân kiên nhẫn]] for students who cannot afford private lessons.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{That said}}, unrestricted use would [[undermine the very purpose of|làm mất đi chính mục đích của]] homework. <<Giải thích>> Writing an essay is valuable precisely because it forces learners to [[organise their ideas|sắp xếp ý tưởng]] and [[wrestle with|vật lộn với]] difficult questions; if a machine produces the text, that [[cognitive effort|nỗ lực tư duy]] disappears. {{Therefore}}, schools need rules that distinguish support from substitution. Students might be allowed to use AI for brainstorming or feedback, {{provided that}} they [[declare how they used it|khai báo mình đã dùng nó như thế nào]] and submit earlier drafts. <<Ví dụ>> Teachers could also [[shift more assessment into the classroom|chuyển nhiều bài kiểm tra đánh giá vào lớp học hơn]], where essays are written by hand or [[under supervision|dưới sự giám sát]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, I largely agree that AI writing tools should be allowed for homework, {{since}} prohibiting them is both unrealistic and [[short-sighted|thiển cận]]. {{However}}, their use must be transparent and limited, so that technology [[supports rather than replaces|hỗ trợ chứ không thay thế]] students' own learning.` },

    { id: 't2-p36', task: 2, ref: 'p36', band: '8.0', words: 293,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As more people [[turn to social media for news|tìm đến mạng xã hội để đọc tin tức]], false information can reach millions of users within hours. I agree that platforms should [[bear some legal responsibility|chịu một phần trách nhiệm pháp lý]] for this content, {{although}} holding them liable for every single post would be neither fair nor workable.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason for regulation is that these companies are not [[neutral noticeboards|những bảng tin trung lập]]. <<Giải thích>> Their algorithms decide which posts are shown to whom, and they tend to [[amplify sensational content|khuếch đại nội dung giật gân]] because it [[generates more engagement|tạo ra nhiều tương tác hơn]] and {{therefore}} more advertising revenue. {{In other words}}, they [[profit directly from|kiếm lợi trực tiếp từ]] the spread of misleading material. <<Ví dụ>> During the pandemic, {{for example}}, fake cures [[circulated widely|lan truyền rộng rãi]] online, and some people [[suffered serious harm|chịu tổn hại nghiêm trọng]] as a result. If a newspaper published such claims, it could be sued, so it seems reasonable to expect similar standards from platforms that [[reach far larger audiences|tiếp cận lượng khán giả lớn hơn nhiều]] than any newspaper ever has.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Nevertheless}}, making companies liable for everything their users write would [[create serious problems of its own|tự nó gây ra những vấn đề nghiêm trọng khác]]. <<Giải thích>> Billions of posts are uploaded every day, and [[fear of prosecution|nỗi sợ bị truy tố]] could push firms to delete anything remotely controversial, which would [[restrict freedom of expression|hạn chế quyền tự do ngôn luận]]. <<Câu chủ đề>> A more sensible approach would be to require platforms to [[act swiftly|hành động nhanh chóng]] once harmful falsehoods are reported, to [[be transparent about|minh bạch về]] how their algorithms work, and to [[face substantial fines|chịu những khoản phạt lớn]] if they repeatedly fail to do so. <<Ví dụ>> The European Union has already moved in this direction, obliging large platforms to [[assess and reduce the risks|đánh giá và giảm thiểu rủi ro]] their services pose to public debate.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} social media firms cannot reasonably check every post in advance, they [[shape what users see|định hình những gì người dùng nhìn thấy]] and should be held accountable for [[how quickly and effectively|nhanh chóng và hiệu quả đến mức nào]] they deal with dangerous misinformation, {{just as}} any other powerful industry is answerable for the harm its products cause.` },

    { id: 't2-p39', task: 2, ref: 'p39', band: '8.0', words: 292,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many cities, including Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City, paying by QR code has become [[second nature|thói quen tự nhiên]] for young people. {{Despite}} the obvious convenience of digital payments, I disagree that cash should be [[phased out entirely|loại bỏ dần cho đến hết]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Admittedly}}, a cashless economy [[offers clear advantages|mang lại những lợi thế rõ ràng]]. <<Giải thích>> Electronic transactions are faster, leave [[a digital record|dấu vết điện tử]] that makes [[tax evasion|trốn thuế]] harder, and reduce the risks of carrying large sums of money. <<Ví dụ>> Sweden, where only a small minority of purchases are now made in cash, has seen a sharp fall in bank robberies. Such a system also [[saves businesses the cost of|giúp doanh nghiệp tiết kiệm chi phí]] handling, storing and transporting notes and coins.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, abolishing cash altogether would [[leave vulnerable groups behind|bỏ lại các nhóm yếu thế phía sau]]. <<Giải thích>> Many elderly people, as well as those in remote rural areas, either lack a smartphone or [[do not feel confident using|không tự tin khi sử dụng]] banking apps, and some low-income workers have no bank account at all. For them, cash is not a habit but [[a lifeline|phương tiện sống còn]]. {{Furthermore}}, a fully digital system is only as reliable as the technology behind it. <<Ví dụ>> A [[power cut|sự cố mất điện]], a cyberattack or [[a network outage|sự cố mất kết nối mạng]] could leave an entire city unable to buy food or medicine, {{whereas}} cash continues to work in any emergency.

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There is {{also}} [[the issue of privacy|vấn đề quyền riêng tư]] to consider. <<Giải thích>> Every electronic payment can be tracked, so a society without cash would give banks and governments [[unprecedented insight into|khả năng nắm bắt chưa từng có về]] citizens' private lives, from their medical expenses to their political donations. Once this information exists, it could easily be misused by hackers or by authorities [[with little regard for|ít coi trọng]] civil liberties.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} digital payments will [[almost certainly|gần như chắc chắn]] [[continue to dominate|tiếp tục chiếm ưu thế]], cash should remain available as [[a safety net|tấm lưới an toàn]] for those who depend on it and as [[a safeguard against|biện pháp phòng vệ trước]] technical failure and excessive surveillance.` },

    { id: 't2-p40', task: 2, ref: 'p40', band: '8.0', words: 286,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Countries differ considerably in [[the age at which|độ tuổi mà]] children start formal lessons in reading, writing and arithmetic. {{While}} an early start has some appeal, I believe young children benefit more from [[play-based learning|học thông qua vui chơi]] until the age of six or seven.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Supporters of early schooling argue that it [[gives children a head start|giúp trẻ có lợi thế xuất phát sớm]]. <<Giải thích>> Young brains [[absorb information remarkably quickly|tiếp thu thông tin nhanh đến kinh ngạc]], so introducing letters and numbers at four or five may help pupils [[keep up with|theo kịp]] increasingly demanding curricula later on. {{In addition}}, school provides [[a structured environment|một môi trường có nề nếp]], which can be particularly valuable for children whose parents work long hours or cannot support their learning at home. <<Ví dụ>> In the UK, {{for instance}}, most children begin school at four, partly to [[narrow the gap|thu hẹp khoảng cách]] between richer and poorer families. Early entry also helps parents [[return to work|quay lại làm việc]] sooner, which benefits the wider economy.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, many educators believe that formal lessons at this age [[do more harm than good|hại nhiều hơn lợi]]. <<Giải thích>> Through unstructured play, children develop [[social and emotional skills|kỹ năng xã hội và cảm xúc]], such as sharing, negotiating and [[managing frustration|kiểm soát sự bực bội]], which [[lay the foundation for|đặt nền móng cho]] later academic success. Pushing them to sit still and complete worksheets too early can {{instead}} [[put them off learning|khiến trẻ chán học]] altogether. By the age of seven, most children are also [[developmentally ready|sẵn sàng về mặt phát triển]] to concentrate for longer periods, so they [[pick up|tiếp thu]] reading and writing far more easily. <<Ví dụ>> Finland, where formal schooling begins at seven, [[has long performed well|từ lâu đã đạt kết quả tốt]] in international assessments, which suggests that a later start [[does not hold children back|không kìm hãm sự phát triển của trẻ]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} early schooling may offer practical benefits for some families, I am convinced that the early years are best spent on [[purposeful play|vui chơi có mục đích]], {{since}} this builds the curiosity and confidence that formal education [[depends on|phụ thuộc vào]].` },

    { id: 't2-p42', task: 2, ref: 'p42', band: '8.0', words: 286,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As governments race to [[cut carbon emissions|cắt giảm khí thải carbon]], there is [[fierce disagreement|sự bất đồng gay gắt]] over whether nuclear power has a place in a clean energy future. In my opinion, renewables should [[receive the lion's share of|nhận phần lớn nhất của]] investment, but nuclear energy still has a limited role to play.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who favour renewables point to their falling costs and lower risks. <<Giải thích>> The price of solar panels and wind turbines has [[plummeted over the past decade|giảm mạnh trong thập kỷ qua]], and such projects can be built in a year or two, {{whereas}} a nuclear plant often takes more than ten years and frequently [[runs vastly over budget|vượt ngân sách rất nhiều]]. {{Moreover}}, nuclear power [[carries the risk of|tiềm ẩn nguy cơ]] accidents and produces [[radioactive waste|chất thải phóng xạ]] that must be stored safely for thousands of years. <<Ví dụ>> The Fukushima disaster in 2011 showed how a single failure can [[displace entire communities|khiến cả cộng đồng phải rời bỏ nơi ở]], and public anxiety after such events often makes new plants [[politically difficult to approve|khó được phê duyệt về mặt chính trị]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, supporters of nuclear energy stress that sunshine and wind are [[intermittent|không liên tục]]. <<Giải thích>> A modern economy needs electricity at night and during calm weather, and large-scale battery storage remains expensive. Nuclear plants {{therefore}} provide [[a stable, low-carbon supply|nguồn cung ổn định, ít phát thải carbon]] that can [[complement|bổ trợ cho]] renewables rather than compete with them. <<Ví dụ>> France, which generates around two thirds of its electricity from nuclear reactors, has [[one of the cleanest power grids|một trong những lưới điện sạch nhất]] in Europe. This [[reliable baseload|nguồn điện nền ổn định]] has allowed the country to keep emissions from electricity generation far below those of neighbours that still [[rely heavily on|phụ thuộc nhiều vào]] coal and gas.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} nuclear power is costly and slow to build, [[dismissing it altogether|gạt bỏ nó hoàn toàn]] would be a mistake. I believe the wisest strategy is to [[prioritise|ưu tiên]] solar and wind energy {{while}} retaining a modest nuclear capacity to [[guarantee a reliable supply|đảm bảo nguồn cung điện ổn định]] [[during the transition|trong giai đoạn chuyển đổi]].` },

    { id: 't2-p45', task: 2, ref: 'p45', band: '8.0', words: 283,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Community service|Hoạt động phục vụ cộng đồng]] is increasingly seen as part of [[a well-rounded education|một nền giáo dục toàn diện]], and some argue that it should be [[made mandatory|trở thành bắt buộc]] for all teenagers [[as a condition of graduating|như một điều kiện để tốt nghiệp]]. {{Although}} I understand this view, I believe that young people gain more when they volunteer [[of their own accord|một cách tự nguyện]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Advocates of compulsory schemes argue that they [[expose teenagers to|cho thanh thiếu niên tiếp xúc với]] realities they would otherwise never see. <<Giải thích>> Helping at a care home or a food bank can [[foster empathy|nuôi dưỡng sự đồng cảm]] and [[a sense of civic responsibility|tinh thần trách nhiệm công dân]], qualities that are not easily taught in a classroom. {{Furthermore}}, making the work compulsory ensures that every student benefits, not only those whose parents [[encourage such activities|khuyến khích những hoạt động như vậy]]. <<Ví dụ>> Some schools in the United States already require a set number of service hours before students can graduate, and supporters claim that many of these students [[go on to|về sau tiếp tục]] volunteer long after the requirement has ended.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, I side with those who believe that volunteering loses much of its value when it is forced. <<Giải thích>> Teenagers who are made to [[clock up hours|tích cho đủ số giờ]] often treat the task as [[a box-ticking exercise|việc làm cho có, cho xong]], doing the minimum and learning little. {{In fact}}, compulsion may [[breed resentment|sinh ra sự bất mãn]] and discourage them from volunteering as adults. {{Besides}}, many young people already [[juggle|xoay xở cùng lúc]] heavy study loads and part-time jobs, so an extra obligation would only [[add to the pressure|tăng thêm áp lực]] they are under. A better approach is to [[make opportunities widely available|tạo cơ hội rộng rãi cho mọi người]] and to recognise students' contributions, for example in university applications.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} compulsory community work aims to build responsible citizens, I believe that genuine commitment [[cannot be imposed|không thể bị áp đặt]], and that encouragement is more effective than obligation. Schools should {{therefore}} [[nurture a genuine interest in|nuôi dưỡng sự quan tâm thực sự đến]] helping others rather than simply counting hours.` },

    { id: 't2-p47', task: 2, ref: 'p47', band: '8.0', words: 285,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> For many teenagers today, the news arrives not in newspapers or bulletins but in sixty-second clips [[scrolled through|được lướt qua]] on a phone. This habit [[raises several concerns|làm dấy lên nhiều mối lo ngại]], but they can be reduced through education and better platform design.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most obvious problem is that short videos [[oversimplify complex issues|đơn giản hóa quá mức các vấn đề phức tạp]]. <<Giải thích>> A topic such as inflation or a war cannot be explained properly in under a minute, so viewers are left with [[a superficial understanding|hiểu biết hời hợt]] and strong opinions based on very little evidence. {{In addition}}, anyone can post such clips, which means that [[unverified claims|những thông tin chưa được kiểm chứng]] often look just as credible as professional journalism. <<Ví dụ>> A dramatic video [[taken out of context|bị cắt khỏi ngữ cảnh]] can be shared millions of times before any correction appears. {{Finally}}, recommendation algorithms tend to show users more of what they already agree with, [[trapping them in echo chambers|nhốt họ trong “buồng vang” thông tin]]. Over time, this can [[harden political divisions|làm chia rẽ chính trị thêm sâu sắc]] and make compromise harder.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several measures could [[mitigate these problems|giảm thiểu những vấn đề này]]. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, schools should teach [[media literacy|năng lực hiểu biết truyền thông]] as a [[core skill|kỹ năng cốt lõi]], training students to [[check the source|kiểm tra nguồn tin]] of a claim and to compare reports from different outlets. {{Secondly}}, platforms could [[attach links to|gắn đường dẫn tới]] reliable background articles beneath news-related videos and clearly label content from anonymous accounts. {{Thirdly}}, viral videos making serious factual claims could be [[temporarily slowed down|tạm thời bị hạn chế lan truyền]] until fact-checkers have reviewed them. {{Meanwhile}}, established news organisations should [[meet young audiences where they are|tiếp cận khán giả trẻ ngay tại nơi họ hiện diện]] by producing accurate, engaging short videos of their own, as several international broadcasters have already begun to do.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, short-form news can [[distort young people's understanding of the world|làm sai lệch hiểu biết của người trẻ về thế giới]], but a combination of critical thinking, responsible platform design and quality journalism can [[turn a weakness into a strength|biến điểm yếu thành điểm mạnh]], [[equipping|trang bị cho]] a generation of digital natives to become better-informed citizens.` },

    { id: 't2-p48', task: 2, ref: 'p48', band: '8.0', words: 281,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Workplace burnout|Tình trạng kiệt sức vì công việc]], a state of [[chronic physical and mental exhaustion|kiệt quệ thể chất và tinh thần kéo dài]], is now reported by employees in almost every industry. The problem [[stems largely from|bắt nguồn phần lớn từ]] the way modern work is organised, and solving it requires action from employers as well as individuals.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> One major cause is that [[the boundary between work and home|ranh giới giữa công việc và gia đình]] has largely disappeared. <<Giải thích>> Because smartphones and messaging apps keep staff [[permanently reachable|lúc nào cũng có thể bị liên lạc]], many people answer emails late at night and never fully [[switch off|thực sự nghỉ ngơi]]. {{Furthermore}}, companies trying to cut costs often [[spread the same workload across fewer staff|dồn cùng một khối lượng công việc cho ít nhân viên hơn]], so employees face [[unrealistic deadlines|những hạn chót phi thực tế]] with little control over how they work. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, nurses and teachers in many countries regularly work unpaid overtime simply to complete their basic duties. {{In addition}}, a culture that [[celebrates long hours|tôn vinh việc làm thêm giờ]] can make employees feel guilty for taking breaks or using their full holiday allowance.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Tackling burnout requires change [[at an organisational level|ở cấp độ tổ chức]], {{rather than}} simply telling workers to be more resilient. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, governments could follow the example of France and Portugal, which have introduced [[a legal right to disconnect|quyền “ngắt kết nối” được pháp luật bảo vệ]] outside working hours, meaning that staff cannot be penalised for ignoring messages in the evening. {{Secondly}}, managers should [[monitor workloads|theo dõi khối lượng công việc]] and [[give employees greater autonomy|trao cho nhân viên nhiều quyền tự chủ hơn]], since people who can organise their own time tend to feel less stressed. <<Giải thích>> {{Finally}}, individuals can [[protect their well-being|bảo vệ sức khỏe thể chất lẫn tinh thần]] by setting firm boundaries, such as turning off notifications in the evening, although this works only if their employers [[respect those limits|tôn trọng những giới hạn đó]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, burnout is largely [[a product of|hệ quả của]] constant connectivity and excessive workloads. No single measure is enough on its own, but clearer legal protections, better management and healthier personal habits together offer the most [[promising way forward|hướng đi đầy triển vọng]].` },

    { id: 't2-p53', task: 2, ref: 'p53', band: '8.0', words: 282,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Food delivery riders, freelance designers and online tutors are all part of [[the rapidly expanding gig economy|nền kinh tế việc làm tự do đang phát triển nhanh chóng]], in which people find short-term work through apps and websites. {{Although}} this model offers genuine freedom, I believe it is [[on balance|xét tổng thể]] a negative development, {{at least}} in its current form.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Nhượng bộ>> [[It must be acknowledged that|Phải thừa nhận rằng]] platform work suits certain people very well. <<Giải thích>> Students, parents with young children and those seeking extra income can choose when and how much they work, and [[the barriers to entry|rào cản gia nhập]] are low. <<Ví dụ>> A university student in Ho Chi Minh City, {{for instance}}, can [[earn a modest income|kiếm một khoản thu nhập khiêm tốn]] by delivering food in the evenings without [[committing to a fixed schedule|bị ràng buộc vào lịch làm việc cố định]]. For such people, the [[flexibility|sự linh hoạt]] of platform work is a genuine advantage rather than a compromise.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, for people who [[depend on this work as their main source of income|phụ thuộc vào công việc này như nguồn thu nhập chính]], the drawbacks are serious. <<Giải thích>> Because they are classified as self-employed, most gig workers [[are not entitled to|không được hưởng]] sick pay, paid holidays or pension contributions, so a single illness or accident can [[plunge them into financial difficulty|đẩy họ vào cảnh khó khăn tài chính]]. {{Moreover}}, their earnings are [[highly unpredictable|rất bấp bênh, khó lường]], as platforms can [[change pay rates overnight|thay đổi mức thù lao chỉ sau một đêm]] or deactivate accounts with little explanation. Many riders work twelve-hour days simply to [[make ends meet|kiếm đủ sống]], with no guarantee of earnings on quiet days. <<Giải thích>> On a wider scale, this [[shifts risk from companies onto individuals|chuyển rủi ro từ doanh nghiệp sang cá nhân]] and [[places additional strain on|gây thêm áp lực lên]] public welfare systems when workers [[fall on hard times|lâm vào cảnh khó khăn]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} the flexibility of platform work [[benefits those who use it as a supplement|có lợi cho những ai coi đó là nguồn thu phụ]], the insecurity it creates makes it a largely negative trend. Governments should {{therefore}} ensure that gig workers [[receive basic protections|được hưởng những quyền lợi bảo vệ cơ bản]] [[comparable to those of|tương đương với quyền lợi của]] ordinary employees.` },

    { id: 't2-p54', task: 2, ref: 'p54', band: '8.0', words: 290,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In Vietnam and much of East Asia, it is common for teenagers to spend their evenings and weekends in [[extra classes|các lớp học thêm]] after a full day at school, often [[at considerable expense to|tốn kém đáng kể cho]] their parents. {{While}} private tutoring can help individual students, I believe its widespread use is [[largely a negative development|phần lớn là một xu hướng tiêu cực]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Admittedly}}, tutoring [[has its merits|cũng có những ưu điểm nhất định]]. <<Giải thích>> In crowded classrooms of forty or more pupils, teachers cannot [[give every child individual attention|quan tâm riêng đến từng em]], so a tutor can [[fill gaps in understanding|lấp những lỗ hổng kiến thức]] and help weaker students [[catch up|bắt kịp các bạn]]. <<Ví dụ>> A student who [[falls behind in|bị tụt lại ở môn]] mathematics, {{for example}}, may regain confidence after a few months of one-to-one lessons. For students preparing for highly competitive entrance exams, extra lessons may also provide [[targeted exam practice|luyện thi có trọng tâm]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, when tutoring becomes the norm rather than the exception, its costs [[outweigh its benefits|lớn hơn lợi ích của nó]]. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, it [[deprives teenagers of|tước đi của thanh thiếu niên]] time for rest, sport and hobbies, which are essential for their physical and mental health, and leaves many of them [[under constant pressure|chịu áp lực thường trực]] to perform. {{Secondly}}, it [[widens the gap between rich and poor|nới rộng khoảng cách giàu nghèo]], {{because}} children from families who cannot afford expensive classes tend to [[fall further behind|ngày càng bị tụt lại]]. <<Giải thích>> {{Finally}}, the culture of extra classes can [[undermine mainstream schooling|làm suy yếu giáo dục chính khóa]]: if parents assume that real learning happens elsewhere, there is less pressure on schools to improve. In some cases, teachers even [[run paid classes for their own pupils|mở lớp dạy thêm có thu phí cho chính học sinh của mình]], creating [[a clear conflict of interest|xung đột lợi ích rõ ràng]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} private tutoring offers valuable support to students who are struggling, its excessive use [[places an unfair burden on|đặt gánh nặng không công bằng lên]] young people and their families. Investment in smaller classes and better teaching would be a far more effective way to [[raise standards|nâng cao chất lượng giáo dục]] for everyone, so that success no longer depends on how much a family can pay.` },

    { id: 't2-p56', task: 2, ref: 'p56', band: '8.0', words: 282,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> It is increasingly common for adults [[well into their twenties or thirties|đã ngoài hai mươi, thậm chí ba mươi tuổi]] to share a home with their parents. This trend is driven mainly by economic pressures, and I believe its effects are [[mixed but broadly positive|lẫn lộn nhưng nhìn chung tích cực]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most important reason is [[the soaring cost of housing|chi phí nhà ở tăng vọt]]. <<Giải thích>> In major cities, rents and property prices have risen far faster than salaries, so many young workers simply cannot afford to [[live independently|sống độc lập]]. {{In addition}}, people now spend longer in education and often begin their careers on [[insecure, short-term contracts|những hợp đồng ngắn hạn, bấp bênh]], which makes it risky to [[take on|gánh vác]] a long-term tenancy or a mortgage. <<Ví dụ>> In Ho Chi Minh City, {{for example}}, renting a small apartment can cost a recent graduate more than half of their monthly salary. Under these circumstances, moving back home is often [[a rational financial decision|một quyết định tài chính hợp lý]] rather than a sign of immaturity.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my view, this development is [[more beneficial than it may first appear|có lợi hơn so với vẻ ban đầu]]. <<Giải thích>> Living at home allows young adults to [[save a substantial deposit|tiết kiệm được một khoản tiền đặt cọc đáng kể]] and avoid debt, giving them a more secure start. It can {{also}} [[strengthen family ties|thắt chặt tình cảm gia đình]], as different generations share household costs and support one another, especially when parents [[grow older|có tuổi]]. <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Admittedly}}, staying in the family home for too long may [[delay the development of|làm chậm sự phát triển của]] practical skills such as budgeting and cooking, and it can [[create friction|gây xích mích]] when adult children and parents have different lifestyles. {{However}}, these problems can be avoided if young people [[contribute to household expenses|đóng góp vào chi tiêu gia đình]] and take on genuine responsibilities.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, the tendency for young adults to remain with their parents [[stems largely from|bắt nguồn phần lớn từ]] unaffordable housing and job insecurity. {{Provided that}} they contribute fairly to family life, I consider this a largely positive trend.` },

    { id: 't2-p57', task: 2, ref: 'p57', band: '8.0', words: 285,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Research suggests that adults in many countries now sleep noticeably less than previous generations did. This is largely [[a consequence of|hệ quả của]] modern technology and working patterns, and it [[takes a heavy toll on|gây tổn hại nặng nề cho]] both personal health and society as a whole.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most obvious cause is the presence of screens in the bedroom. <<Giải thích>> Many people [[scroll through social media|lướt mạng xã hội]] or watch videos late into the night, and the light from these devices [[interferes with|cản trở]] the body's natural sleep signals. {{Moreover}}, [[long working hours|giờ làm việc kéo dài]] and lengthy commutes leave little time for rest, {{while}} the expectation of being available online means that some people [[check work messages|kiểm tra tin nhắn công việc]] right up until bedtime. <<Ví dụ>> Students, {{similarly}}, often [[stay up late|thức khuya]] revising for exams or playing games, [[sacrificing sleep|hy sinh giấc ngủ]] for short-term goals. Shift work and the growth of [[round-the-clock services|các dịch vụ hoạt động suốt ngày đêm]] have also [[disrupted|làm xáo trộn]] the natural rhythms of millions of workers.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The effects of [[chronic sleep deprivation|tình trạng thiếu ngủ kéo dài]] are serious. <<Giải thích>> For individuals, a lack of sleep [[impairs concentration and memory|làm suy giảm khả năng tập trung và trí nhớ]] and is linked to a higher risk of obesity, heart disease and depression. {{As a result}}, people who are constantly tired tend to be less productive and more irritable. <<Giải thích>> For society, the consequences are equally significant. Exhausted employees make more mistakes and take more sick days, which [[costs economies billions|gây thiệt hại hàng tỉ cho các nền kinh tế]] every year. <<Ví dụ>> Drowsy driving is also [[a major cause of|một nguyên nhân chính gây ra]] road accidents, putting the lives of other road users at risk, and the extra [[strain on health services|gánh nặng lên hệ thống y tế]] is ultimately paid for by taxpayers.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, people are sleeping less mainly because of digital devices and demanding schedules. {{Since}} inadequate sleep [[undermines|làm suy yếu]] health, productivity and public safety, it deserves to be [[treated as a public health issue|được xem là một vấn đề sức khỏe cộng đồng]] {{rather than}} a purely private matter.` },
  ]);
