  g.MODELS = (g.MODELS || []).concat([
    { id: 't2-p81', task: 2, ref: 'p81', band: '8.0', words: 273,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In a world where information is [[freely available|có sẵn miễn phí]] at the touch of a screen, the ability to find and judge knowledge may matter more than the knowledge itself. I largely agree that schools should [[give priority to|ưu tiên]] independent learning, although facts should not be [[neglected altogether|bị bỏ qua hoàn toàn]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason for this view is that facts quickly become [[outdated|lỗi thời]]. <<Giải thích>> A student who has memorised a long list of dates or formulas gains little if they cannot [[locate and evaluate|tìm và đánh giá]] new information once the textbook is closed. {{By contrast}}, learners who know how to plan their study, ask good questions and [[check their sources|kiểm tra nguồn tin]] can [[adapt to|thích nghi với]] any subject throughout their lives. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a graduate who must learn a new software system at work will succeed if they have the habit of [[self-directed study|tự học có định hướng]], whatever they were taught at school.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{However}}, independent learning cannot [[take place in a vacuum|diễn ra trong khoảng trống]]. Students need a [[solid base of knowledge|nền tảng kiến thức vững chắc]] in order to ask meaningful questions and to notice when a source is [[misleading|gây hiểu lầm]]. <<Giải thích>> A pupil with no historical background, {{for instance}}, will struggle to judge whether an online article about a war is [[balanced|cân bằng, khách quan]]. {{Therefore}}, schools should teach core facts {{while}} gradually [[handing over responsibility|trao dần trách nhiệm]] to students through projects and research tasks. This balance is already visible in some schools, where teachers set short research projects each term and then discuss the results with the class, so that pupils practise [[thinking critically|tư duy phản biện]] while still absorbing essential content.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, I agree that schools should place more emphasis on learning skills than on [[rote memorisation|học thuộc lòng]], {{provided that}} a basic framework of knowledge is still [[taught alongside|được dạy song song]] these skills.` },
    { id: 't2-p82', task: 2, ref: 'p82', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Many city centres are [[choked by|ngột ngạt vì]] traffic, and some planners now propose removing private cars completely. While I accept that this would bring clear benefits, I believe a total ban is [[too extreme|quá cực đoan]], and I only partly agree with the proposal.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are strong arguments in favour of restricting cars. <<Giải thích>> Vehicles are a leading source of [[air and noise pollution|ô nhiễm không khí và tiếng ồn]], and they take up enormous amounts of public space that could be used for [[parks, cafés and wide pavements|công viên, quán cà phê và vỉa hè rộng]]. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, when several European cities [[closed their main squares to traffic|đóng cửa các quảng trường chính với xe cộ]], local shops reported more customers and residents enjoyed cleaner air. {{Moreover}}, fewer cars means fewer road accidents, which makes the centre safer for children and older people.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Nevertheless}}, an absolute ban would create real problems. <<Giải thích>> Disabled people and the elderly may depend on cars to [[reach the centre|đến được trung tâm]], and businesses need deliveries and [[emergency services|dịch vụ cấp cứu]] must always have access. A ban would also fail unless the city already has [[a reliable and affordable public transport network|một mạng lưới giao thông công cộng đáng tin cậy và giá phải chăng]]; otherwise, residents would simply be [[cut off|bị cô lập]] from jobs and services. A sensible first step would be to close the busiest streets at peak hours and to measure the effect on air quality and local trade before extending the restrictions, {{so that}} decisions are based on evidence {{rather than}} on opinion.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, cities should certainly [[reduce the dominance of|giảm sự thống trị của]] private cars, {{but}} a more realistic approach is to charge fees, create car-free zones at busy times and invest heavily in public transport {{rather than}} to ban cars outright.` },
    { id: 't2-p83', task: 2, ref: 'p83', band: '8.0', words: 251,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Diets that are high in salt and sugar are [[closely linked to|liên quan chặt chẽ tới]] heart disease and diabetes. I strongly agree that governments should [[compel|buộc]] manufacturers to reduce these ingredients, because individual choice alone has [[failed to solve|không giải quyết được]] the problem.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most convincing reason is that consumers cannot easily control what is already in their food. <<Giải thích>> Much of the salt and sugar people eat is [[hidden in processed products|ẩn trong thực phẩm chế biến sẵn]] such as bread, sauces and breakfast cereals, so even careful shoppers [[consume more than they realise|ăn nhiều hơn họ nghĩ]]. {{As a result}}, health campaigns that [[urge people to eat less|kêu gọi người dân ăn ít đi]] have limited effect. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, when the United Kingdom set voluntary salt targets for manufacturers, average salt intake fell noticeably without customers having to change their habits.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Reducing these ingredients also brings wider social benefits. <<Giải thích>> Diet-related illness places a heavy [[burden on health services|gánh nặng lên hệ thống y tế]], and lower rates of disease would save public money that could be spent on other priorities. <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Admittedly}}, some companies argue that customers will reject products that taste less sweet or salty. {{However}}, if the rules apply to every manufacturer, no company is at a [[competitive disadvantage|bất lợi về cạnh tranh]], and people's taste preferences can [[adjust gradually|điều chỉnh dần dần]] over time. It is also worth noting that clear food labelling would support such rules, because shoppers could then [[compare products|so sánh sản phẩm]] easily and reward the manufacturers that have already [[reformulated|điều chỉnh công thức]] their recipes.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{since}} so much salt and sugar is added before food reaches the shopper, I believe legislation is a fair and effective way to [[safeguard public health|bảo vệ sức khỏe cộng đồng]].` },
    { id: 't2-p84', task: 2, ref: 'p84', band: '8.0', words: 253,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Software now allows companies to record keystrokes, emails and browsing history on work devices. I disagree that employers should be allowed to monitor everything, {{although}} some [[reasonable oversight|sự giám sát hợp lý]] is justified.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Employers do have legitimate reasons to keep an eye on work computers. <<Giải thích>> Companies hold sensitive data belonging to clients, and a careless or dishonest employee could cause [[serious security breaches|vi phạm an ninh nghiêm trọng]]. Monitoring can also [[deter|ngăn chặn]] misconduct such as harassment by email. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a bank may reasonably scan outgoing messages to stop confidential customer details from being leaked.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, monitoring everything goes too far. <<Giải thích>> Constant surveillance is an [[invasion of privacy|sự xâm phạm quyền riêng tư]], since employees often need to check personal messages or book a medical appointment during the day. Knowing that every click is recorded can also create stress and [[erode trust|làm xói mòn lòng tin]] between staff and management, which in turn [[undermines|làm suy yếu]] morale and creativity. <<Ví dụ>> Studies of call centres suggest that workers who feel permanently watched are more likely to leave their jobs, which is costly for the employer. A fair policy would therefore limit monitoring to work-related activity, explain what is recorded and why, and give staff a separate way of [[handling personal matters|giải quyết việc cá nhân]], which would protect both security and dignity. {{In this way}}, trust and security can coexist. Clear written policies also help employers avoid legal disputes.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, companies should be permitted to monitor only what is necessary to protect their data and legal interests, {{provided that}} staff are told clearly what is recorded. Total surveillance is neither fair nor [[productive in the long run|hiệu quả về lâu dài]].` },
    { id: 't2-p85', task: 2, ref: 'p85', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Companies around the world are developing cars that can drive themselves without human input. I agree that this technology has the potential to make roads safer, {{though}} the benefits will not appear [[overnight|ngay lập tức]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main argument for safety is that most accidents are caused by human error. <<Giải thích>> Drivers become tired, distracted by phones or impaired by alcohol, whereas a computer-controlled vehicle [[never loses concentration|không bao giờ mất tập trung]] and can react in a fraction of a second. {{In addition}}, such vehicles can [[communicate with each other|giao tiếp với nhau]], so they can warn one another about hazards ahead. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, if one car detects ice on a bend, every vehicle approaching that bend could slow down automatically.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Nhượng bộ>> {{However}}, the technology is not yet perfect. <<Giải thích>> Sensors can [[misread|đọc sai]] unusual situations such as heavy snow or a cyclist [[swerving unexpectedly|đột ngột lạng sang bên]], and a handful of serious crashes involving test vehicles have shown that software errors can be [[fatal|gây tử vong]]. Safety will therefore improve only if manufacturers are [[subject to strict testing standards|chịu các tiêu chuẩn kiểm định nghiêm ngặt]] and if human drivers and automated cars share the road [[carefully|cẩn thận]] during the transition. Public confidence is another factor, since many people will only trust driverless cars once the statistics clearly show that they [[cause fewer accidents|gây ra ít tai nạn hơn]] than human drivers, and this will take years of careful data collection. {{For now}}, human oversight will remain necessary. Insurance and legal rules will need to be updated as well.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{because}} human error is the root of most collisions, self-driving vehicles could eventually make roads far safer, {{provided that}} the technology is thoroughly tested and regulated.` },
    { id: 't2-p86', task: 2, ref: 'p86', band: '8.0', words: 276,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Choosing an employer is one of the most important decisions in a career. While large international companies attract many graduates, others prefer the [[familiar atmosphere|bầu không khí quen thuộc]] of a small local business. Both options have merit, but I personally favour the second for the early stage of a career.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Large multinational firms offer several clear advantages. <<Giải thích>> They usually pay higher salaries, provide structured training and give employees a chance to [[work abroad|làm việc ở nước ngoài]] or move between departments. A well-known name on a CV also [[opens doors|mở ra cơ hội]] later on. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a young engineer at a global manufacturer may be sent on courses in several countries and meet colleagues from many cultures.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, small businesses offer a different kind of experience. <<Giải thích>> Staff are often given [[broader responsibilities|trách nhiệm rộng hơn]] much earlier, because there are fewer people to share the work, and they can see the direct effect of their efforts on the company. Relationships with managers are also closer, so good ideas are heard quickly. <<Ví dụ>> A junior employee at a local design studio might {{therefore}} deal with clients, plan projects and learn several skills within a single year, {{whereas}} a counterpart in a large firm may do one narrow task. In practice, many people combine the two experiences by starting in a small firm to [[build a broad skill set|xây dựng bộ kỹ năng đa dạng]] and then moving to a larger organisation once they have a clear idea of what they want to specialise in.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, big companies provide [[security and prestige|sự ổn định và uy tín]], {{while}} small businesses give faster [[personal development|sự phát triển cá nhân]]. {{For this reason}}, I believe the latter is the better place to begin, {{although}} the right choice ultimately depends on one's personality and goals.` },
    { id: 't2-p87', task: 2, ref: 'p87', band: '8.0', words: 260,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Parents and policy makers often argue about whether smaller classes lead to better results. Some people believe that they do, {{while}} others claim that the teacher is the deciding factor. In my opinion, teacher quality is more important, although class size still has some influence.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who favour small classes argue that pupils receive more individual attention. <<Giải thích>> With fewer students, a teacher can notice who is [[falling behind|tụt lại phía sau]], give detailed feedback and [[tailor|điều chỉnh cho phù hợp]] explanations to each child. {{In addition}}, discipline is easier to maintain, so more time is spent on learning. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a language teacher with twelve students can let everyone speak in every lesson, which is impossible with forty.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, I believe the teacher's skill matters more. <<Giải thích>> An inspiring and well-trained teacher knows how to explain difficult ideas clearly and [[motivate|tạo động lực cho]] students, and these abilities [[make a difference|tạo ra sự khác biệt]] whatever the size of the room. By contrast, a weak teacher will not [[achieve good results|đạt được kết quả tốt]] even with a handful of pupils. <<Ví dụ>> Several countries with consistently high test scores have fairly large classes but invest heavily in teacher training and [[professional development|phát triển chuyên môn]]. Of course, the ideal arrangement is a skilled teacher with a manageable class, but when budgets are limited, spending on the quality of teaching is likely to bring a [[better return|hiệu quả cao hơn]] than hiring extra staff. {{In short}}, good teaching is the foundation of any successful class.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, small classes can help, {{but}} I believe that governments would see greater improvements by [[recruiting and training excellent teachers|tuyển dụng và đào tạo giáo viên xuất sắc]] than by simply reducing the number of pupils in each room.` },
    { id: 't2-p88', task: 2, ref: 'p88', band: '8.0', words: 257,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Healthcare costs are rising in almost every country, which has [[reopened the debate|làm sống lại cuộc tranh luận]] over who should pay. Some people argue that the state should cover all treatment, while others believe individuals should bear the cost. I agree with the first view, with some reservations.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are strong reasons why governments should fund medical care. <<Giải thích>> Illness can strike anyone, and expensive treatment could [[push families into poverty|đẩy các gia đình vào cảnh nghèo]] if they had to pay it themselves. Free care also encourages people to see a doctor early, which is cheaper than treating advanced disease. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, patients in countries with universal health systems are more likely to attend regular check-ups, so illnesses such as cancer are often found at an earlier stage.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, those who support individual payment have a point. <<Giải thích>> A system funded entirely by taxes can become [[overstretched|quá tải]], leading to long waiting lists, and some people argue that charging patients would discourage [[unnecessary visits|những lần khám không cần thiết]]. Such an approach, {{however}}, would [[penalise|gây thiệt thòi cho]] the poor and the chronically ill, who need care most. A better compromise might be a small fee for non-urgent services combined with free emergency and long-term treatment. Countries that have adopted this mixed model show that it is possible to keep costs under control {{while}} ensuring that no one is [[turned away|bị từ chối]] because of an inability to pay. {{Overall}}, a shared system is fairer and more efficient.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} charging patients may reduce misuse, I believe that governments should pay for essential healthcare so that everyone has [[equal access to treatment|cơ hội tiếp cận điều trị bình đẳng]], {{regardless of}} income.` },
    { id: 't2-p89', task: 2, ref: 'p89', band: '8.0', words: 254,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Museums preserve a nation's history and art, yet they are expensive to run. Opinions differ on whether they should be free or charge an entry fee. Although fees can raise money, I believe that museums should be free.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who favour charging argue that museums need a reliable source of income. <<Giải thích>> Looking after collections, running exhibitions and employing specialist staff are all costly, and ticket sales can reduce the [[burden on taxpayers|gánh nặng lên người đóng thuế]]. Some people also feel that visitors [[value|trân trọng]] an experience more when they have paid for it. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, several private galleries use ticket revenue to fund new acquisitions and restoration work.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, I believe free entry is better for society. <<Giải thích>> Culture and history belong to everyone, and a fee can [[exclude|loại trừ]] low-income families and students, who may have the most to gain from visiting. Free admission also attracts a wider and more diverse audience, including people who would never have thought of going. <<Ví dụ>> When the United Kingdom abolished entry charges at its national museums, visitor numbers rose sharply, and many of the new visitors came from groups that rarely attended before. Museums can still raise funds through gift shops, cafés and voluntary donations. Many museums have also found that visitors who come for free are more likely to return, buy souvenirs or become members later, so the lost ticket income is often [[partly recovered|được bù đắp phần nào]] in other ways.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} entry fees bring in money, free access serves the public interest by making culture available to all, and I therefore support it.` },
    { id: 't2-p90', task: 2, ref: 'p90', band: '8.0', words: 257,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As conflict and persecution force millions of people to flee their homes, countries disagree about their responsibility to help. Some argue that rich nations have a [[moral duty|nghĩa vụ đạo đức]] to offer shelter, {{while}} others believe governments must put their own citizens first. I mostly support the first view, {{although}} the second raises valid concerns.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Wealthy countries have both the means and the obligation to help. <<Giải thích>> They have the housing, healthcare and education systems needed to [[support newcomers|hỗ trợ những người mới đến]], and international law recognises the right of people in danger to seek protection. {{Moreover}}, refugees often contribute to their new societies once they have settled. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, many refugees have started businesses or filled shortages of doctors and builders, which benefits the whole economy.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, those who prioritise citizens raise practical worries. <<Giải thích>> A sudden arrival of large numbers of people can put [[pressure on|tạo áp lực lên]] housing, schools and public services, particularly in areas that are already poor, and local residents may feel that their needs are being ignored. These concerns are real, {{but}} they can be addressed through careful planning and fair [[burden-sharing|chia sẻ gánh nặng]] between countries, {{rather than}} by closing the door. Public attitudes tend to improve when people see that refugees are [[treated fairly|được đối xử công bằng]] and that the support given to newcomers does not come at the expense of services for existing residents. {{In the end}}, compassion and careful planning can go together.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, I believe rich countries should accept refugees, {{provided that}} they plan carefully and [[invest in integration|đầu tư vào việc hòa nhập]], so that both newcomers and existing residents can benefit.` },
    { id: 't2-p91', task: 2, ref: 'p91', band: '8.0', words: 260,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Millions of tonnes of plastic enter the world's rivers and seas every year, [[harming marine life|gây hại cho sinh vật biển]] and damaging coastal economies. This essay will examine the main causes of the problem and suggest several measures to reduce it.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The pollution has two main causes. <<Giải thích>> Firstly, the world produces vast quantities of [[disposable|dùng một lần]] plastic, such as bags, bottles and food packaging, which is used for minutes but lasts for centuries. Secondly, waste management is inadequate in many regions. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, where rubbish is not collected regularly or recycling facilities are [[lacking|thiếu]], plastic is dumped or [[washed into|bị cuốn vào]] drains and eventually reaches the sea. {{In addition}}, consumers often [[lack the habit|thiếu thói quen]] of sorting their waste.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several solutions could help. <<Giải thích>> Governments can [[tax or ban|đánh thuế hoặc cấm]] the most wasteful items, as many countries have done with plastic bags, and require manufacturers to [[pay for the collection and recycling|chi trả cho việc thu gom và tái chế]] of their packaging. {{At the same time}}, investment in waste collection and recycling plants is essential, especially in fast-growing cities. <<Ví dụ>> Deposit schemes, in which shoppers receive a small refund for returning bottles, have achieved return rates above ninety per cent in some countries. Schools and media campaigns can also [[change attitudes|thay đổi thái độ]] towards single-use products. Finally, companies that invent biodegradable alternatives deserve [[government support|sự hỗ trợ của chính phủ]], because lasting progress requires replacing plastic where possible, {{not just}} collecting it after it has been used. {{Only then}} will the flow of plastic into the oceans begin to slow.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, plastic pollution results from [[excessive production|sản xuất quá mức]] and poor waste management, {{and}} it can be reduced through regulation, better infrastructure and public education.` },
    { id: 't2-p92', task: 2, ref: 'p92', band: '8.0', words: 261,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Many people complain that they can no longer concentrate on a task for more than a few minutes because their phones keep buzzing. This essay will explain why this happens and suggest practical ways of regaining [[focus|sự tập trung]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The problem is partly a matter of design. <<Giải thích>> Apps are built to [[capture attention|thu hút sự chú ý]], using alerts, red badges and endless feeds that trigger a small reward each time we check them, so people develop a habit of [[reaching for their phones|với tay lấy điện thoại]] without thinking. {{Furthermore}}, many workplaces expect rapid replies to messages, which makes it hard to [[switch off|ngắt kết nối]]. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, research suggests that after an interruption, it can take over twenty minutes to return fully to a demanding task.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Fortunately, there are several solutions. <<Giải thích>> Individuals can turn off non-essential notifications, keep their phones in another room while working and set specific times for checking messages. Employers can help by agreeing that staff are [[not expected to respond|không bị yêu cầu phải trả lời]] immediately outside urgent situations. <<Ví dụ>> Some companies have introduced "quiet hours" in the afternoon during which no meetings or messages are allowed, and employees report being more productive. Technology companies could also be encouraged to design [[less intrusive|ít gây phiền hơn]] apps. Setting such limits is not a sign of weakness: it is a recognition that attention is a [[limited resource|nguồn lực có hạn]] and that protecting it is as important as managing time or money. {{In practice}}, a few simple rules can restore a great deal of focus.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, constant notifications are a result of [[attention-grabbing design|thiết kế nhằm thu hút sự chú ý]] and workplace culture, {{but}} individuals and organisations can reduce their impact with simple, deliberate habits.` },
    { id: 't2-p93', task: 2, ref: 'p93', band: '8.0', words: 264,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> It seems paradoxical that people living in crowded cities often feel lonely, yet this is increasingly reported. This essay will outline the main reasons for this feeling and propose some ways of reducing it.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several factors explain urban loneliness. <<Giải thích>> City life is fast and [[impersonal|thiếu tính cá nhân]]: people commute long hours, work long days and rarely know their neighbours. Many have also moved far from family and old friends for work, so they have to start from scratch. {{In addition}}, social media may replace face-to-face contact with [[superficial|hời hợt]] online interaction, which leaves people feeling [[isolated|cô lập]] even when they are connected. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a young professional in a new city may chat online every evening yet go days without a real conversation.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are, {{however}}, practical ways to tackle the problem. <<Giải thích>> Local authorities can create [[shared spaces|không gian chung]] such as community centres, parks and neighbourhood events that encourage people to meet. Employers can offer flexible hours so that staff have time for social activities, and schools and charities can organise volunteer projects. <<Ví dụ>> Some cities run "befriending" schemes that pair elderly residents with young volunteers, which [[benefits both groups|có lợi cho cả hai nhóm]]. Individuals, {{for their part}}, can make an effort to join clubs and get to know their neighbours. Over time, these small connections can grow into genuine friendships, which show that the city, {{far from}} being the cause of loneliness, can also be the place where it is solved.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, loneliness in cities stems from busy lifestyles, [[weak community ties|mối liên kết cộng đồng yếu]] and digital substitutes for real contact, {{and}} it can be reduced through better public spaces and a little personal initiative.` },
    { id: 't2-p94', task: 2, ref: 'p94', band: '8.0', words: 254,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many countries, a significant proportion of students leave university without a degree, which wastes both money and potential. This essay will discuss the main reasons for this trend and suggest some ways of addressing it.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reasons are financial and academic. <<Giải thích>> Tuition fees and living costs are high, so many students must work long hours, leaving little time to study, and some eventually conclude that they can no longer afford to continue. Others [[choose the wrong course|chọn nhầm ngành học]] because they lacked guidance at school and later lose motivation. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a student who enrolled in engineering because of family pressure may struggle with the content and decide to leave after the first year. {{In addition}}, some students feel isolated or overwhelmed by the [[transition|sự chuyển đổi]] from school to university.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several measures could reduce dropout rates. <<Giải thích>> Governments and universities could offer more scholarships, grants and flexible [[part-time options|hình thức học bán thời gian]] so that money is less of an obstacle. Better career advice before enrolment would help young people choose suitable courses, {{while}} mentoring and counselling services during the first year could [[identify struggling students|phát hiện những sinh viên gặp khó khăn]] early. <<Ví dụ>> Universities that have introduced personal tutors for first-year students have reported noticeably higher completion rates. Employers can contribute as well by offering degree apprenticeships, so that students who find a traditional course unsuitable can still gain qualifications {{while}} earning a salary and [[gaining practical experience|tích lũy kinh nghiệm thực tế]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, students leave university mainly because of financial pressure and [[poor course choices|lựa chọn ngành học không phù hợp]], {{but}} better funding, advice and support could help many more of them to graduate.` },
    { id: 't2-p95', task: 2, ref: 'p95', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many countries, a small group of people owns a growing share of the wealth, {{while}} millions struggle to afford basic needs. This essay will explain the problems this [[inequality|bất bình đẳng]] creates and suggest what governments can do about it.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> A large wealth gap has serious consequences. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, it limits opportunity: children from poor families often attend weaker schools and have less access to healthcare, so they find it hard to [[move up the social ladder|vươn lên trong xã hội]]. {{Secondly}}, it can damage social cohesion, because people who feel excluded may lose trust in institutions and become [[more likely to turn to crime|dễ có nguy cơ phạm tội hơn]]. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, studies have found that countries with greater inequality tend to have higher crime rates and poorer public health.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Governments have several tools to narrow the gap. <<Giải thích>> A [[progressive tax system|hệ thống thuế lũy tiến]], in which high earners pay a larger percentage, can fund public services, {{while}} raising the minimum wage helps those on low incomes. Investing in free, good-quality education and training is equally important, because it gives disadvantaged people the skills to [[earn a better living|kiếm sống khá hơn]]. <<Ví dụ>> Several northern European countries combine high taxes with strong public services and have some of the smallest wealth gaps in the world. It is important that these measures are [[well targeted|được nhắm đúng đối tượng]] and monitored, {{because}} poorly designed policies may be costly without actually helping those in greatest need. {{In the long run}}, a fairer society is also a more stable one.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{because}} inequality harms opportunity and social stability, governments should respond with fairer taxation, decent wages and investment in education.` },
    { id: 't2-p96', task: 2, ref: 'p96', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Solo travel, once considered unusual, is [[growing in popularity|ngày càng phổ biến]], particularly among young adults. In my view, the advantages of travelling alone [[outweigh|lớn hơn]] the disadvantages, {{although}} there are some real drawbacks.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main advantage is freedom. <<Giải thích>> A solo traveller can choose where to go, how long to stay and what to do without having to [[compromise|thỏa hiệp]] with companions who want different things. Travelling alone also [[builds confidence and independence|xây dựng sự tự tin và tính độc lập]], since travellers must solve problems such as missed trains or language barriers themselves. {{Furthermore}}, people on their own are often more open to [[meeting locals|gặp gỡ người địa phương]] and other travellers. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a lone backpacker may be invited to a family meal or join a group hiking trip, experiences that are less likely when one is part of a large party.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, there are disadvantages. <<Giải thích>> Safety is the most common worry, as a solo traveller has no one to help in an emergency or to watch their luggage. Costs can also be higher, because expenses such as hotel rooms and taxis are not shared, and some people may feel lonely during long journeys. {{Nevertheless}}, these problems can be reduced by good planning, such as booking safe accommodation and staying in touch with family. Travellers can also reduce the loneliness of a solo trip by staying in hostels, joining organised tours for part of the journey or using apps that help them [[connect with fellow travellers|kết nối với những du khách khác]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} travelling alone involves some risks and expense, I believe that the independence and personal growth it offers make it a worthwhile choice.` },
    { id: 't2-p97', task: 2, ref: 'p97', band: '8.0', words: 260,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Thanks to improved internet technology, many universities now allow students to earn a full degree without ever entering a classroom. I believe that the advantages of this development are greater than the drawbacks, {{provided that}} the courses are well designed.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main benefit of online degrees is accessibility. <<Giải thích>> Students who live far from a university, who work full time or who have family responsibilities can study [[at their own pace|theo tốc độ của riêng mình]] and from home, which would otherwise be impossible. Online courses are also usually cheaper, because students save on accommodation and travel. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a nurse in a rural area can complete a management degree in the evenings while continuing to work, {{and}} a student abroad can attend a prestigious university [[without relocating|mà không cần chuyển chỗ ở]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, studying online has real disadvantages. <<Giải thích>> Learners miss the face-to-face contact, group discussions and [[campus life|đời sống sinh viên]] that help many people to stay motivated, and some find it hard to [[maintain self-discipline|duy trì tính kỷ luật bản thân]] without fixed timetables. Subjects that require laboratory work or practical training are also difficult to teach remotely. {{As a result}}, dropout rates on online programmes are often higher. {{Even so}}, universities can [[mitigate|giảm thiểu]] these problems by offering live sessions, tutor support and occasional practical workshops. The quality of the awarding university is also crucial, so students should [[check|kiểm tra]] that a programme is properly accredited before they commit time and money to it. {{In short}}, quality assurance is what separates a valuable degree from a worthless one.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, online degrees [[widen access|mở rộng cơ hội tiếp cận]] to higher education, and with proper support their advantages outweigh their limitations.` },
    { id: 't2-p98', task: 2, ref: 'p98', band: '8.0', words: 258,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As life expectancy rises and pension systems come under pressure, many governments are raising the age at which people retire. I believe this is a positive development overall, {{although}} it must be handled carefully.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The benefits are both economic and personal. <<Giải thích>> {{From an economic point of view}}, a larger working population pays more tax and reduces the strain on state pensions, which are hard to fund when the number of retired people is [[growing faster than|tăng nhanh hơn]] the number of workers. {{On a personal level}}, many older people are healthy and wish to remain active, and continuing to work gives them income, purpose and social contact. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, experienced staff can pass their skills on to younger colleagues, {{and}} studies suggest that people who work longer have a lower risk of [[loneliness and cognitive decline|sự cô đơn và suy giảm nhận thức]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, there are drawbacks. <<Giải thích>> Not everyone is able to continue: people in physically demanding jobs such as construction may be exhausted or injured by their sixties, and a rigid retirement age would be [[unfair to them|bất công với họ]]. Longer careers may also reduce opportunities for young job seekers. {{Therefore}}, governments should allow [[flexible retirement|nghỉ hưu linh hoạt]] and provide support for those who cannot keep working. In the long term, governments could also encourage employers to retrain older staff and to offer [[phased retirement|nghỉ hưu theo giai đoạn]], so that experience is not lost overnight. {{In this way}}, longer careers can be both productive and fair.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, later retirement helps public finances and can benefit older people themselves, but it should be [[accompanied by|đi kèm với]] flexibility and protection for those in demanding jobs.` },
    { id: 't2-p99', task: 2, ref: 'p99', band: '8.0', words: 273,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Watching other people play video games online, through platforms such as live streaming sites, has become a daily habit for millions. This essay will suggest why it is so popular and argue that it is largely harmless when done in moderation.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are several reasons for its popularity. <<Giải thích>> Firstly, viewers enjoy the skill and entertainment of expert players, much as they enjoy watching professional sport, and they can learn tactics that help them to improve their own play. Secondly, live streams create a sense of [[community|cộng đồng]], because viewers can chat with the streamer and one another in real time. {{In addition}}, the content is free and available at any hour. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, a lonely teenager might [[feel part of a group|cảm thấy mình thuộc về một nhóm]] by joining a favourite streamer's chat every evening.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> I believe that this pastime is mostly harmless, {{provided that}} it is kept in balance. <<Giải thích>> It can be relaxing, social and even educational, and it is no more passive than watching television. <<Nhượng bộ>> {{Nevertheless}}, there are risks if viewers spend [[excessive amounts of time|quá nhiều thời gian]] online, because this can lead to less sleep, less exercise and neglected schoolwork, and some streams expose young people to [[inappropriate language|ngôn ngữ không phù hợp]] or pressure to spend money. Parents and viewers should therefore [[set sensible limits|đặt ra những giới hạn hợp lý]]. Overall, spectators who treat streams as one entertainment option among many are unlikely to come to any harm, and for some they are even a useful [[source of inspiration|nguồn cảm hứng]] for their own hobbies.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, game streams are popular because they combine entertainment, learning and community, and they are a harmless way to spend time {{as long as}} they do not [[crowd out|chiếm chỗ của]] other important activities.` },
    { id: 't2-p100', task: 2, ref: 'p100', band: '8.0', words: 263,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> A growing number of people are giving up meat, or eating much less of it. This essay will explain the main motives behind this decision and argue that governments should encourage it, {{though}} not force it.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> People choose not to eat meat for two main reasons. <<Giải thích>> The first is environmental: livestock farming produces a large share of greenhouse gases and requires huge areas of land and water, so a plant-based diet has a smaller [[carbon footprint|dấu chân carbon]]. The second is ethical, as many people object to the conditions in which farm animals are kept and prefer not to [[take part in|tham gia vào]] what they regard as cruelty. {{In addition}}, some people are motivated by health, since diets rich in vegetables are linked to lower rates of heart disease. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a student may stop eating meat after learning that beef production uses far more water than growing vegetables.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> I believe governments should encourage, {{rather than}} compel, this change. <<Giải thích>> They could include more plant-based meals in schools and hospitals, support farmers who grow vegetables and legumes, and run campaigns that inform the public about the [[impact of food choices|tác động của lựa chọn thực phẩm]]. <<Nhượng bộ>> {{However}}, forbidding meat would be unpopular and would [[interfere with|can thiệp vào]] personal freedom and cultural traditions. Gentle incentives are more likely to produce lasting change. Better labelling and affordable alternatives such as high-quality vegetarian products would help people to [[make informed choices|đưa ra lựa chọn có hiểu biết]] without feeling that they are being told what to eat.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, people avoid meat mainly for environmental and ethical reasons, and governments can help them by making [[sustainable options|lựa chọn bền vững]] cheaper, more available and better understood.` },
  ]);
